#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116416 :tutorial/content "To plot the graph of $y=-2(3^x),$ we take the curve $y=2(3^x)$ and reflect it in the $x$-axis, as shown below:\n\n![](images/61/6130842f788070c7e97fe82698b701293af681a6aba3acfce0f4dde269cddbcf.png)\n\nThis transformation is called a **reflection in the $\\boldsymbol{x}$-axis**, and it occurs because the output $y$-values of the function $y=2(3^x)$ are made negative by the transformation.\n\nIf we combine vertical translations with reflection, we get a general form\n\n$$\ny = -a(b^x) + k.\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a reflected exponential graph $y=-a(b^x)$ that is translated $k$ units up." :tutorial/id #uuid "73a5f011-6c32-5204-b086-8c7c7a7bdead" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14028 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
